#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179673 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179529 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5b5c517-8e13-5b70-bd1f-15ae495f0632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/id #uuid "1cadcaa9-876e-56cb-b8b3-17695827c4f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-7035" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7a6e67435aab40d5bf3630c66f79241008357e1549b1c77b4c0bdfea2b453883.png)\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C \\dfrac{y\\,\\mathrm{d}x - x\\,\\mathrm{d}y}{x^2+y^2},$ where $C$ is a part of the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $\\left(0,2\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that a parametrization for the given path is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{′}(t)\\,i+{y}^{′}(t)\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y\\,dx-x\\,dy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{y\\cdot {x}^{′}-x\\cdot {y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}},dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{-4{sin}^{2}⁡t-4{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{-4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}{4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,dt \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,dt \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5b5c517-8e13-5b70-bd1f-15ae495f0632" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050616}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557849 :question-generator/id #uuid "480672cd-2500-5116-be47-cbc1ab04be13" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186179673.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5b5c517-8e13-5b70-bd1f-15ae495f0632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02791ce3-0e59-59cb-a7c2-9c1f5f9b8efe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256804} {:answer/id #uuid "190adee9-da90-5ae0-b72f-1d54cbec3732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256997} {:answer/id #uuid "560421a3-f826-5226-b6d1-b81db072eecf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257522} {:answer/id #uuid "7390b29f-af98-55d8-908c-23b7a22da5d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257794} {:answer/id #uuid "b236cd5d-f995-5fc3-910f-4a9dd21013f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t+{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b236cd5d-f995-5fc3-910f-4a9dd21013f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t+{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258334} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c7b60de-da7e-5307-9f2b-34e67ae5cd00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec525dce-ff06-5bce-974f-75c3d14b6b37" :question/math-academy-id "q-124597" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx-{x}^{2}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx-{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({y}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}-{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-{sin}^{3}⁡t-{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t+{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5b5c517-8e13-5b70-bd1f-15ae495f0632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50e89690-a473-5e96-8e1b-8772141217ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349180} {:answer/id #uuid "530d4756-be46-5443-808a-7585bbe7565c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349205} {:answer/id #uuid "6697a40a-e34b-5bb9-950b-de39a8750a4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349386} {:answer/id #uuid "921c24c0-b579-5b2b-8d7f-6191bf8532b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349754} {:answer/id #uuid "9aead026-b2ca-55fe-850d-efdababe6aa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "921c24c0-b579-5b2b-8d7f-6191bf8532b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349754} :answer-field/id #uuid "245516cf-1ad0-5aae-a56c-6eb5c3eb71f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "233cf6a6-4ea2-585c-8c92-fd68b4e1c273" :question/math-academy-id "q-81192" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y\\,dy-x\\,dx}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y\\,dy-x\\,dx}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{y\\cdot {y}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t-x\\cdot {x}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{y\\cdot {y}^{′}-x\\cdot {x}^{′}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[-1-1] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5b5c517-8e13-5b70-bd1f-15ae495f0632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f4f537-9c38-5a4a-92d9-1fc05c60f90d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256834} {:answer/id #uuid "3789ffb2-b7e7-5c19-9e46-46878e9dd27c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257270} {:answer/id #uuid "78bd4c3d-cccd-5c94-8184-aae6764245be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-2{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257849} {:answer/id #uuid "df481504-80af-5bb2-8167-dfe689532510" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t(1-4{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258727} {:answer/id #uuid "e25e2064-e1d7-50b0-b597-8c407a11946b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e25e2064-e1d7-50b0-b597-8c407a11946b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258751} :answer-field/id #uuid "046799fa-5877-570c-92f0-0954916f5fcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1324f71e-2d99-5801-9cdc-c3861396a17d" :question/math-academy-id "q-124656" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(-2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-16{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5b5c517-8e13-5b70-bd1f-15ae495f0632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77262323-79ed-5ade-a88d-cfe620b7da6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257835} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f4a4ec-6fb3-5b3a-ace0-98c820d9456a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-1\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258256} {:answer/id #uuid "ce873cc3-03d3-531f-9ee9-9641247e9f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+1\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258576} {:answer/id #uuid "da94ceb2-a1b8-5f7d-817e-d3c142cc2c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258692} {:answer/id #uuid "dd7f1f33-0822-5dd8-b24e-299b64b487d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-2\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8f4a4ec-6fb3-5b3a-ace0-98c820d9456a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-1\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258256} :answer-field/id #uuid "665ec369-2b3a-59ec-a54c-232cc0215c3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b45898cb-232a-5e7b-9fc7-75fcf27a9882" :question/math-academy-id "q-124657" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{2}{y}\\,dx+y{e}^{x}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{2}{y}\\,dx+y{e}^{x}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2}{y}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+y{e}^{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{2}{y}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+y{e}^{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-1\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5b5c517-8e13-5b70-bd1f-15ae495f0632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f6d91d8-d6dd-52c9-b3ff-b1db44329a0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257620} {:answer/id #uuid "6266e64e-03c1-58e9-a2a7-faa1e9d1ab4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\pi -5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257649} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e0d134-f8c3-5cad-825a-eca5a50c39ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi +1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258774} {:answer/id #uuid "eef0270e-6352-5435-b70f-a6965924b8db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi -5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258853} {:answer/id #uuid "fddbbdf7-66ee-5acd-97fd-6d8474436d61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6266e64e-03c1-58e9-a2a7-faa1e9d1ab4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\pi -5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257649} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4c70434-b863-58cf-ba58-7f3a3ae42742" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3189421a-c22a-5fa2-87db-a7498ce87212" :question/math-academy-id "q-124661" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(-2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{′}(t)\\,i+{y}^{′}(t)\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(x-y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x+y)\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }((x-y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+(x+y)\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6{sin}^{2}⁡t+6{cos}^{2}⁡t+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+6\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+6t]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[(-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )+6\\pi )-(-\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+3\\pi )] \\\\  & =(-\\frac{5}{4}+6\\pi )-(\\frac{5}{4}+3\\pi ) \\\\  & =3\\pi -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi -5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259706 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5b5c517-8e13-5b70-bd1f-15ae495f0632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c3f67e-c143-52cf-8d04-0883a4d5a256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256943} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfb3475-8c2e-59e8-84f2-1ebef1519964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257605} {:answer/id #uuid "79b7785e-2e75-5831-91d7-a32ea05f26cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257857} {:answer/id #uuid "a627c339-0874-52fc-ab69-4b8d01f804f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258229} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c4c7b4-80c1-5190-8730-555b2288af36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi +1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a627c339-0874-52fc-ab69-4b8d01f804f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258229} :answer-field/id #uuid "39dbfdbb-3be4-5981-9a19-60472431c20b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e2357f5-0311-5b8a-87b7-eaae763c04c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124665" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}dx+\\frac{2}{x}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(-1,0)$ to the point $(0,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}dx+\\frac{2}{x}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{2}{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2}{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6t]_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+9\\pi )-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +6\\pi ) \\\\  & =(0+9\\pi )-(-1+6\\pi ) \\\\  & =3\\pi +1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5b5c517-8e13-5b70-bd1f-15ae495f0632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2f1d0a0-f937-5c5a-a906-b8d8c3476536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387535} {:answer/id #uuid "b7352c23-717f-5780-b0d0-69655ec1cf5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387536} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9c4e1a-9de0-5b54-9a84-bd61bf918f52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387537} {:answer/id #uuid "8321670f-4321-5b31-a978-0d03d440b8cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387538} {:answer/id #uuid "6b7e8a42-2b6d-59b6-8e6b-34f626db24de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa9c4e1a-9de0-5b54-9a84-bd61bf918f52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387537} :answer-field/id #uuid "46e3edf1-c65d-5b40-9b5a-4947c9652286" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "35a5240c-df73-5f77-b045-605d4d50b078" :question/math-academy-id "q-124651" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,dx+xy\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $1,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,dx+xy\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+xy\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+xy\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-8{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5b5c517-8e13-5b70-bd1f-15ae495f0632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e75bdd4-9ffa-598b-ba2b-3e4718966455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+3\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387542} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b5e560-c25e-5180-91e9-4dd26cebdf25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387543} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5e8954-0ec8-5e2c-87e8-00cd2ba07083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387544} {:answer/id #uuid "e56c1b23-1bc1-5e37-920c-d6b5c4c83b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+1\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387545} {:answer/id #uuid "96172456-0f12-5f8e-95b8-f6b631ca3655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+3\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96172456-0f12-5f8e-95b8-f6b631ca3655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+3\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387546} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f4769e4-af1d-5493-a3fe-c583f362931e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8b59c59-a390-5b22-8421-70bf95742c06" :question/math-academy-id "q-124653" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}({e}^{x}+y)\\,dx-x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(0,\\sqrt{3})$ to the point $B(-\\sqrt{3},0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({e}^{x}+y)\\,dx-x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }({e}^{x}+y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(({e}^{x}+y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}-x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }({e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-3{sin}^{2}⁡t-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+3\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5b5c517-8e13-5b70-bd1f-15ae495f0632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57571e56-49bb-5855-a1ea-93ab592af29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}6{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387549} {:answer/id #uuid "dee85688-f7f4-5420-abcb-2c8946c6128c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387550} {:answer/id #uuid "581fc888-b8c4-5b98-a662-97d6df2c1c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}6-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387551} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa25052-4bcd-5645-84a9-cb44c291ba75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387552} {:answer/id #uuid "179cd5d2-4e71-5c6d-adbe-d8d33387b74f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}3-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "581fc888-b8c4-5b98-a662-97d6df2c1c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}6-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387551} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab73ef72-cd0a-53ca-954d-b2e9e89157ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4adcf258-fc9a-5300-865b-c8efb5f76002" :question/math-academy-id "q-124655" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x-2y)\\,dx+2x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(0,3)$ to the point $B(0,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-2y)\\,dx+2x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(x-2y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+2x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}((x-2y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+2x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6{sin}^{2}⁡t+6{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}6-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5b5c517-8e13-5b70-bd1f-15ae495f0632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f548fd7-8b35-5ec3-9cec-fbc79515f307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415406} {:answer/id #uuid "68411306-3576-579b-8a21-34c1ab7c2b84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415416} {:answer/id #uuid "910075a4-9923-557c-b9e3-c1e2ff793ed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415422} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d88b10-d2ad-5c06-9db7-9b7365276f6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415426} {:answer/id #uuid "f7735ce7-7b0f-5b5a-82f6-7e753a067e1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f548fd7-8b35-5ec3-9cec-fbc79515f307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415406} :answer-field/id #uuid "0df18aab-7e84-55d8-803f-e0704648d1b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "228970be-ca08-547c-9937-b7e76a0b4145" :question/math-academy-id "q-81186" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $B(0,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the parametrization of the curve $C.$ Since it's a circle if radius $2$ centered at the origin, the curve $C$ can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve we have that $x(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(-4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t{|}_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )] \\\\  & =2(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5b5c517-8e13-5b70-bd1f-15ae495f0632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Along Circles and Ellipses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "256764ed-4369-50dc-930e-662876f022a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415404} {:answer/id #uuid "4130f50c-9761-535e-832c-969370eba9c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415410} {:answer/id #uuid "56de169b-e6cc-53f1-9778-d57c45a41155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415413} {:answer/id #uuid "72db92dd-d3bb-5244-